with the private sector so weakened by  this recession the federal government is  the only entity left with the resources  to jolt our economy back into life the  eight hundred and forty billion dollar  stimulus bill pump money into every  community in the country to pay for  things like transit infrastructure tax  breaks weatherizing homes and keeping  teachers on the payroll economist  Veronique doraji is a senior research  fellow at the Mercatus Center at George  Mason University and an expert on the  stimulus the administration said that  unemployment would reach likely eight  point eight percent without the stimulus  but that was the stimulus he would never  reach that level and in fact it would  start going down really quickly the  reality is it actually has gone way past  the eight point eight percent and hover  above nine percent for the last two  years reason TV did a case study looking  at how the stimulus money was spent in  one community in Obama's backyard the  town of Silver Spring Maryland a  commuter suburb of Washington DC even if  you were to believe that government  spending can trigger economic growth the  money is never spent in a way that is  consistent with the condition laid out  by the kansian for it to be efficient  nearly 400,000 men and women will go to  work rebuilding our crumbling roads and  bridges repairing our faulty dams and  levees bringing critical broadband  connections to businesses and homes in  nearly every community in America in  fact the u.s. construction industry shed  about a million jobs in the year after  the stimulus was passed and two and a  half years later the industry still  hasn't recovered Maryland's specialty  trade construction industry which  handles big infrastructure projects has  shed about 8,000 jobs since the stimulus  was passed meanwhile stimulus money has  steadily paid the salaries of only 600  construction workers in Maryland since  the middle of 2009 so why did the  stimulus create so few construction jobs  for one thing many the project's paid  for by the stimulus would have happened  anyway the resurfacing of this highway  in Silver Spring is the first stimulus  project in the nation to get shovels in  the ground it was also fairly typical it  was a routine repair job instead of  actually thinking about where the money  we need to go to hire those itll  resources infrastructure spending when  two jobs that were already lined up or  it went to actually pay the salary you  were already on the job but Maryland got  seven hundred and seventy 1 million  stimulus dollars for transit  infrastructure projects so why didn't  all that extra money have a bigger  effect on the economy  well Maryland Governor Martin O'Malley  cut infrastructure spending more than  enough to offset any gains from the  stimulus see before the stimulus  Maryland's Transportation Trust Fund  paid for highway repairs by collecting a  special gas tax and other user fees  after the stimulus money was available  Governor O'Malley rated the fund to the  tune of 861 million dollars over the  next three years to pay for things that  have nothing to do with infrastructure  even if you account for the stimulus  money which totaled 771 million dollars  state funding for transit infrastructure  still saw a net decrease of ninety  million dollars after the stimulus was  passed large government contractors got  a disproportionate share of stimulus  funds according to an August 2009 report  and government executives the fastest  way to spend government money is to  spend it on channels that already exist  give it to gum  contractors that you've already hired to  just do more of the same thing and that  goes against the philosophy of the  stimulus that's what happened in  downtown Silver Spring were a hundred  and thirty eight million dollars in  stimulus grants and contracts went to 46  organizations but just three firms got  51% of the money these three firms were  major government contractors before the  stimulus was signed in fact the stimulus  money they received pales in comparison  to what they were already getting from  other government contracts take  Palladium partners a communications firm  in Silver Spring it's received ninety  seven and a half million dollars in  government contracts over the past  twelve years after the stimulus was  passed the National Institute of Health  which is pleadians biggest client gave  the firm 795,000 stimulus dollars to  disseminate information about how the  NIH was spending stimulus money with the  project 80 percent done the firm has  also been well paid for what it's  accomplished it built a website that  aggregates press releases and reprinted  news items from other websites so far  it's contributed 29 original articles to  the site averaging just over a thousand  words each it's allowed the firm to hire  two new employees neither of whom was  unemployed before coming to Palladian a  california solar energy company called  Solyndra burned through more than 500  million dollars in federal loan money  taxpayer money only to declared  bankruptcy earlier this month the  Solyndra bankruptcy may be the biggest  green jobs fiasco paid for by the  stimulus but there were others the  stimulus also financed a five hundred  million dollar program to train and  recruit 80,000 people to work in green  jobs with the grant more than half over  the program has found jobs for only  8,000 people in downtown Silver Spring a  union backed organization called the  Transportation Learning Center  participated in that program received a  five million dollar grant the money was  to teach new green job skills to public  transit employees and to recruit and  train three thousand ninety five people  to work in the transit industry but a  year into its grant the Transportation  Learning Center got permission to back  out of its commitment to place people in  jobs because they've changed  economic conditions so now it's using  the entire grant to